      "notes": [
        "**שֶׁל** (*shel*) means \"of\" or \"belonging to\". To say \"my book\", Hebrew says **הַסֵּפֶר שֶׁלִּי** — literally \"the-book of-me\". Note that הַ־ is required: if something belongs to someone, it's automatically definite.",
        "⚠️ **אַבָּא and אִמָּא don't take הַ־.** You say אַבָּא שֶׁלִּי (not הָאַבָּא שֶׁלִּי) — they're treated like names, just as English says \"Dad\" rather than \"the dad\". Other family words still take הַ־: הָאָח שֶׁלִּי.",
        "**שֶׁלִּי, שֶׁלְּךָ, שֶׁלָּךְ …** — שֶׁל takes person endings, the same way prepositions take endings later in unit 14. The full set is in the next box. Think of it as one word that turns into my, your, his, her, our, their.",
        "⚠️ **שֶׁלְּךָ (to a man) vs שֶׁלָּךְ (to a woman)** — both are spelled שלך without niqqud. This is the second pair (after אתה/את) that looks identical on paper and sounds different.",
        "**יֵשׁ לִי** (*yesh li*) = \"I have\", literally **\"there is to me\"**. Hebrew has no verb \"to have\" at all. For now, learn it as a chunk: יֵשׁ לִי (I have), יֵשׁ לְךָ / יֵשׁ לָךְ (you have). Unit 14 takes the whole machine apart.",
        "**Masculine plural ־ִים, feminine plural ־וֹת** — and verbs go plural too: הֵם גָּרִים (they live). English marks the plural on the noun; Hebrew marks it on **every** word in the sentence.",
        "⚠️ **אָח simply means \"brother\" — Hebrew has no words for \"older\" or \"younger\" siblings built in.** When it matters, people add הַגָּדוֹל (the big one) or הַקָּטָן (the little one): הָאָח הַגָּדוֹל שֶׁלִּי = my big (older) brother.",
        "**בֶּן שְׁמוֹנִים וְחָמֵשׁ** — age is expressed with בֶּן (\"son\") for males and בַּת (\"daughter\") for females. \"I'm 25\" = אֲנִי בֶּן עֶשְׂרִים וְחָמֵשׁ. Literally \"I son twenty and-five\". Numbers come in unit 5."
      ],
